---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/art-history/advanced/modern-and-contemporary-art">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an advanced lesson in Art History, and it assumes the ladder below it: the
    foundational lesson <em>A Walk Through Western Art</em>, then <em>Renaissance and
    Baroque</em>, <em>Impressionism to Expressionism</em> and <em>Art Beyond the West</em>.
    Those lessons established the conventions this chapter watches being dismantled —
    perspective, finish, depiction, the Western centre of gravity — one movement at a time.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="true or false — 'I could do that'" widget="judge" config={mcaSkillJudge} />
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>narrate the modernist cascade — Cubism, abstraction, Dada, constructivism — as a chain of arguments rather than a list of styles;</li>
    <li>explain how material choices — papier collé, the readymade, drip, silkscreen — became the argument itself;</li>
    <li>analyse who a work addresses, from the Bauhaus chair to Abramović's audience-as-material;</li>
    <li>read borrowings and the global turn honestly, through japonisme, Picasso's sources and the 1984 MoMA controversy;</li>
    <li>apply several interpretive frameworks to one object and say what each reveals and misses.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Modern and contemporary art is the <strong>relentless interrogation of its own
    premises</strong> — from Cubism's shattered space to conceptual art's dematerialised
    object. It is the hardest period to love casually and the most rewarding to read: every move
    is an argument with every previous move.
  </p>
  <p>
    The period runs roughly from the 1880s to the present, and its logic is cumulative rather
    than sequential. Cubism answers photography (what can painting do that the camera cannot?);
    abstraction answers Cubism (if depiction is optional, take the next step); Dada answers the
    First World War (if civilisation produced that, its art deserves sabotage); Pop answers
    Abstract Expressionism's seriousness with the supermarket aisle; conceptual art answers the
    commodity with the idea; and the global contemporary answers the whole Euro-American story
    by simply refusing to be its footnote. Read in this order, the wildest-looking works become
    moves in an intelligible game.
  </p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="rebuild the cascade — each move answers the last" widget="order" config={mcaCascadeOrder} />

  <Widget no="1" caption="the cascade, New York, dematerialisation, the expanded field" widget="equationStep" config={modernCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the four steps form a causal chain, not a timeline to
    memorise: each movement answers the one before it — photography forces Cubism's
    question, abstraction radicalises it, Dada sabotages the premises, and the contemporary
    field widens the game to installation, video and identity. Track the question each step
    asks rather than the names it lists.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the period's logic is cumulative — every wildest-looking work
    is a move in an intelligible game. The next section shows the game's engine: material
    and process becoming the argument themselves.
  </p>

  <h2>Materials, processes and techniques: the medium becomes the message</h2>
  <p>
    Before modernism, technique largely served an agreed task: paint well enough and you had
    earned your subject. The twentieth century inverted this — <strong>the choice of material
    and process became the argument itself</strong>. Braque's <em>Fruit Dish and Glass</em>
    (1912) pastes a real, printed scrap of wood-grain wallpaper into a charcoal drawing: the
    first <em>papier collé</em>. The mechanism matters. That scrap is simultaneously flat paper
    on a flat surface (painting telling the truth about itself) and a fake table top (painting
    lying as usual) — the contradiction is the content. And because the scrap carries actual
    newsprint, the outside world, with its dates and prices, physically enters the picture for
    the first time since antiquity.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="bet first — what does the real scrap do?" widget="predict" config={mcaPapierPredict} />
  <p>
    Each subsequent decade re-ran the same move with a new material. Duchamp's
    <em>Fountain</em> (1917) is a mass-produced porcelain urinal, signed "R. Mutt" and submitted
    to the Society of Independent Artists — a show that proudly claimed to reject nothing; its
    board refused the piece anyway, which proved the work's point better than any display
    could. Pollock, from 1947, abandoned the easel: unprimed canvas on the floor, industrial
    house paint, and a stick; the painting becomes a record of a body moving in time, which is
    why the films of him working matter as much as the canvases. Warhol, from 1962, used the
    silkscreen — the mechanical reproduction technique — to paint
    <em>Campbell's Soup Cans</em>, thirty-two canvases, one per variety, in the machine look the
    era actually saw on shelves. Video, performance, land art and, eventually, software each
    arrived as the next "what if the material were X?" question.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="pair each artist with the material that became the argument" widget="match" config={mcaMaterialMatch} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "I could do that" — the claim that technique-free equals
  skill-free. Pollock's drips are controlled layering built over months (he said he could
  control the flow, and the films show it); Warhol's screens are tuned, re-registered and
  re-inked by hand. The skill moved from the wrist to the decision: choosing what counts as
  the work is itself a craft, and you did not think of it first.</p>

  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> each decade re-ran one move — the material choice became the
    argument — until skill migrated from the wrist to the decision. The next section asks
    the question every one of those decisions implies: who is the work for?
  </p>

  <h2>Purpose and audience: who is the art for?</h2>
  <p>
    For centuries the answers were stable — church, crown, then the bourgeois salon. Modern art
    split the question open. The Bauhaus (founded Weimar 1919) wanted design fused with
    industry so the audience was everyone who sat in a chair; the Soviet Constructivists
    (Rodchenko, Lissitzky) treated the poster as the painting's true heir because a thousand
    copies beat one altarpiece; the Mexican muralists (Rivera, Orozco, Siqueiros) took the
    medium to the state's own walls, where its publics could not opt out — Rivera's
    <em>Detroit Industry</em> cycle (1932–33) addressed factory workers in a factory, while his
    Rockefeller Center mural was destroyed in 1934 over the portrait of Lenin that kept
    appearing in it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The contemporary version sharpens the question: sometimes the audience is the material.
    Marina Abramović's <em>Rhythm 0</em> (Naples, 1974) placed seventy-two objects on a table —
    feather, honey, scissors, a loaded gun — with the instruction that she would accept whatever
    the public did with them for six hours; what the public chose to do is the work, and it is
    not a comfortable read. Richard Serra's <em>Tilted Arc</em> (1981) curved twelve feet of
    COR-TEN steel across a New York federal plaza until office workers petitioned for its
    removal; hearings followed, and in 1989 it was cut up in storage. Serra's claim was precise:
    site-specific sculpture cannot be relocated without being destroyed — which means the
    <em>audience's refusal</em> becomes part of the piece's history. Contemporary purpose is
    rarely decoration; it is an offer the viewer must answer.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="file the encounters — material or objector?" widget="sort-buckets" config={mcaAudienceBuckets} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that contemporary artists despise audiences, since the work
  can be difficult. Difficulty is usually the opposite of contempt — the work trusts you with a
  problem instead of feeding you a result. The honest test is not "did I enjoy it?" but "did it
  do to me whatever it set out to do?"</p>

  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> modern art split the old audience — church, crown, salon — into
    publics that must answer the work, and sometimes the audience becomes the material
    itself. Next, the canon's blind side: where its celebrated ruptures borrowed from, and
    which direction the traffic was recorded in.
  </p>

  <h2>Interactions within and across cultures: the global turn</h2>
  <p>
    The canon's celebrated ruptures were also borrowings, and the discipline now reads them
    honestly. Picasso's <em>Les Demoiselles d'Avignon</em> (1907) owes its masked faces to
    African carving (he knew works at the Trocadéro) and Iberian reliefs — an appropriation that
    revolutionised Western painting while the cultures that made the source objects were, at
    that exact moment, having them taken. Decades earlier, <em>japonisme</em> had already done
    its work: Van Gogh copied Hiroshige's prints in 1887, learning flat colour and cropped
    framing that Impressionism absorbed wholesale. "Primitivism" is the period's own word, and
    part of reading modern art is noticing what it was blind to.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="run the hang — three ways to label a borrowing" widget="branch" config={mcaHangBranch} />
  <p>
    The field formalised that critique in a museum itself. The Museum of Modern Art's 1984
    exhibition <em>"Primitivism" in 20th Century Art</em> hung modernist masterpieces beside
    the masks and figures that had fed them, under the banner of "affinity." The critic Thomas
    McEvilley's celebrated review asked what the hang refused to: the affinities ran in one
    direction only, the loans were displayed as anonymous raw material rather than as works by
    artists, and the exhibition's scholarship treated non-Western objects as fuel. The show is
    now studied less for its contents than for its consequences — the moment the discipline's
    own institutions staged, and thereby crystallised, the postcolonial critique that reshaped
    collecting, programming and art-historical writing over the following decades. Learning to
    read a museum hang as an argument, and an argument as a document of its time, is one of
    this unit's transferable skills.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="drag the divider — one exhibition, two readings" widget="compare" config={mcaAffinityCompare} />
  <p>
    The deeper correction is that abstraction was never a Western monopoly. Islamic
    calligraphic traditions had privileged non-figurative order for a millennium; mandala
    geometry built worlds from circles and squares; and when Papunya Tula artists began
    painting on board and canvas in 1971, Western Desert Australian painting entered the
    contemporary conversation on its own terms — systems of mapping Country that make
    Mondrian's grids look local rather than universal. By the 1990s the institutional map
    followed: the Venice Biennale (since 1895) and Documenta (Kassel, since 1955) were joined
    by São Paulo (1951), Gwangju (1995) and Shanghai (1996). Okwui Enwezor's <em>Documenta11</em>
    (2002) went furthest, holding discussion platforms across several continents before the
    Kassel exhibition itself — the mega-exhibition reconceived as a worldwide argument rather
    than a European showcase.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="call the magnitude — a century of biennales" widget="estimate" config={mcaBiennaleEstimate} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that "global contemporary art" means Western art finally
  exported everywhere. The traffic runs both ways and always did — the museum canon simply
  recorded one direction for a century. A Kusama infinity room, an El Anatsui metal tapestry
  and a Ai Weiwei installation are not answering Paris; they are answering their own
  genealogies, with the market lesson's money flowing toward all of them.</p>

  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the global turn corrected the canon's direction of traffic, and
    the institutions themselves — biennales, mega-exhibitions, a museum hang read as an
    argument — became the critique's venue. The last section supplies the reading
    instruments: the frameworks professional interpretation runs on.
  </p>

  <h2>Theories and interpretations: one work, read four ways</h2>
  <p>
    Advanced reading means knowing that <strong>interpretation is done by frameworks, not just
    by eyes</strong>. Formalism (Clement Greenberg, 1940s–50s) evaluates art by the purity of
    its medium — painting should be about flatness and pigment, so it likes Pollock and
    distrusts anything theatrical. Social history (the Marxist line, from T. J. Clark's
    <em>The Painting of Modern Life</em>, 1984) reads art as ideology's witness — it asks what
    social interest the soup can serves. Feminist art history began with one devastating
    question, Linda Nochlin's "Why Have There Been No Great Women Artists?" (1970–71), whose
    answer — the institutions, not the biology — rewrote the canon's admissions criteria.
    Postcolonial theory (from Edward Said's <em>Orientalism</em>, 1978) reads the Western gaze
    on the rest of the world as a subject in its own right. And institutional theory (Arthur
    Danto's "The Artworld", 1964; George Dickie) explains the strangest fact of all: that
    framing something as art — in a gallery, by an artist, before an informed audience — can
    make it so.
  </p>
  <p>
    Try the frameworks on one object: Duchamp's <em>Fountain</em>. Formalism has nothing to say
    (there is no hand in it); social history reads a urinal priced as art as a joke about
    commodities that the market later absorbed; feminist and queer readings note the
    pseudonymous "R. Mutt" and the alter ego Rrose Sélavy (from 1921) — identity itself played
    as a readymade; institutional theory simply says the piece proves its own thesis. The
    object did not change. The reading did.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that theory "ruins" direct looking. Untheorised looking is
  just looking with the local framework's defaults switched on and hidden. Naming your
  framework is what lets you change it — and changing frameworks, as the four readings show,
  is where the richness lives.</p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Recall: what does the wallpaper scrap in Braque's Fruit Dish and Glass do that painted imitation cannot, and why does that make it an argument?</li>
    <li>Application: apply two of the lesson's frameworks to Warhol's Campbell's Soup Cans and state what each reveals.</li>
    <li>Why-reasoning: why is "I could do that" the wrong objection to Pollock or Warhol, given where the lesson says the skill moved?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) The scrap is simultaneously real paper — painting telling
    the truth about its own flatness — and a fake table top, painting lying as usual: a
    contradiction painted imitation cannot stage. And because it is printed wallpaper, the
    outside world physically enters the picture. The material is the argument. (2)
    Formalism reads the silkscreen's flatness as painting admitting it is a surface; social
    history asks what interest the supermarket aesthetic serves, mirroring consumer
    culture deadpan; institutional theory notes that gallery framing is what makes soup
    labels art. Any two, applied rather than named, do the job. (3) Because the skill moved
    from the wrist to the decision: Pollock's drips are months of controlled layering,
    Warhol's screens are hand-tuned and re-registered, and above all, choosing what counts
    as the work is itself a craft — and the person objecting did not think of it first.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Modern art is cumulative argument: each movement answers the previous one, from Cubism's answer to photography to the contemporary refusal of footnotes.</li>
    <li>Technique became the thesis — papier collé, the readymade, drip and silkscreen each made the material the argument.</li>
    <li>Audience is a design decision: industry, posters, state walls, and works whose material is the public's own behaviour.</li>
    <li>The canon's ruptures were also borrowings; part of reading modern art is noticing what primitivism was blind to.</li>
    <li>Abstraction was never a Western monopoly; the global turn re-plumbed the traffic in both directions.</li>
    <li>Interpretation runs on frameworks — formalist, social, feminist, postcolonial, institutional — and naming yours is what lets you change it.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The sibling advanced lesson <em>Art Market and Museums</em> follows the objects this
    chapter made valuable into galleries, auction rooms and museum boards, and the expert
    tier's <em>Art Historiography and Method</em> turns the frameworks section into a
    discipline of its own.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Three frontiers organise the expert layer. <strong>Dematerialisation:</strong> Lucy Lippard
    named it in "The Dematerialization of Art" (1968) and curated the so-called "number shows",
    indexed by population; Sol LeWitt's wall drawings took the logic furthest — he sold
    instructions, other hands executed them, and the museum repaints the work on each reinstall
    without anyone calling it a copy. <strong>The market's absorption:</strong> conceptual
    gestures that were meant to escape the commodity became its luxury tier — Damien Hirst's
    diamond-encrusted <em>For the Love of God</em> (2007) carried a £50 million asking price,
    and Jeff Koons built a factory-grade studio practice whose products are indexes of belief
    in art itself. <strong>The digital turn:</strong> Beeple's NFT collage realised $69.3
    million at Christie's in March 2021 — the auction house that sold old masters selling a
    token — before the sector crashed in 2022; and AI-generated images now pose the same
    question Duchamp posed, relocated from the urinal to the prompt: if choosing and framing
    make art, who is the artist when a model does the rendering?
    <strong>The identity turn:</strong> alongside the market's rise, the 1980s–90s organised
    artistic politics into the institutions themselves — the Guerrilla Girls (founded 1985),
    gorilla-masked statisticians of the canon, published their famous poster asking whether
    women had to be naked to get into the museum, counting far fewer women artists than female
    nudes in the modern galleries; Gran Fury turned ACT UP's urgency into public design, and
    institutional critique moved from the gallery wall to the board structure — a direct line
    to today's museum-governance debates. The cascade in the widget
    above ends where the story actually is: mid-argument.
  </p>
